{"id":"8979273d-a70b-40b4-a37a-cf9671eee899","object":"chat.completion","created":1784983084,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\":\"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    core = case['core_distance']\\n    distances = case['distances']\\n    edges = []\\n    for u, v, d in distances:\\n        if u > v:\\n            u, v = v, u\\n        mr = max(core[u], core[v], d)\\n        edges.append((mr, u, v))\\n    edges.sort(key=lambda e: (e[0], e[1], e[2]))\\n    parent = {node: node for node in nodes}\\n    rank = {node: 0 for node in nodes}\\n    def find(x):\\n        while parent[x] != x:\\n            parent[x] = parent[parent[x]]\\n            x = parent[x]\\n        return x\\n    def union(x, y):\\n        rx, ry = find(x), find(y)\\n        if rx == ry:\\n            return False\\n        if rank[rx] < rank[ry]:\\n            parent[rx] = ry\\n        elif rank[rx] > rank[ry]:\\n            parent[ry] = rx\\n        else:\\n            parent[ry] = rx\\n            rank[rx] += 1\\n        return True\\n    mst_edges = []\\n    total_weight = 0.0\\n    for mr, u, v in edges:\\n        if union(u, v):\\n            mst_edges.append([u, v, mr])\\n            total_weight += mr\\n            if len(mst_edges) == len(nodes) - 1:\\n                break\\n    if len(mst_edges) != len(nodes) - 1:\\n        raise ValueError('Disconnected graph')\\n    return {'mst_edges': mst_edges, 'total_weight': total_weight}\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to produce Python source code for solve(case) that implements the HDBSCAN mutual reachability MST. The output must follow the schema: {\"mst_edges\": list of [node1, node2, weight], \"total_weight\": number}. The input case has nodes list, core_distance dict, distances list of [node1, node2, distance]. For each pair, compute mutual reachability = max(core_dist[node1], core_dist[node2], distance). Then build MST using Kruskal: sort edges by weight, then by node IDs to break ties. Use union-find. Return edges list and sum of weights. Ensure deterministic. Return as JSON-serializable.\n\nWe must not mutate case. Use only standard lib. No imports beyond json maybe, but we don't need json; solve returns dict. We'll implement solve(case) with internal functions.\n\nEdge case: ensure nodes are strings, distances are floats. Build adjacency for all pairs.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\n- nodes = case[\"nodes\"]\n- core = case[\"core_distance\"]\n- distances = case[\"distances\"]\n- Build list of edges: for each (u,v,d) in distances, compute mr = max(core[u], core[v], d). Append (mr, u, v)\n- Sort edges: key = (mr, u, v) where u<v? Actually we need canonical order: we can sort tuple (mr, min(u,v), max(u,v)). But Kruskal edge order: sort by weight, then by endpoints. So key = (mr, u, v) where u<v for deterministic tie-breaking. Ensure u and v are sorted.\n- Union-Find with path compression and union by rank.\n- Initialize parent = {node: node}, rank = {node:0}\n- mst_edges = []\n- total_weight = 0.0\n- For each (mr, u, v) in sorted edges:\n   - if find(u) != find(v):\n       - union(u,v)\n       - mst_edges.append([u, v, mr])\n       - total_weight += mr\n       - if len(mst_edges) == len(nodes)-1: break\n- After loop, if len(mst_edges) != len(nodes)-1: raise ValueError(\"Disconnected graph\")\n- Return {\"mst_edges\": mst_edges, \"total_weight\": total_weight}\n\nThat's it.\n\nNow produce final answer as JSON with key \"implementation\" and value as Python source string escaped. Ensure no extra text.\n\nWe'll output exactly: {\"implementation\": \"<escaped python code>\"}\n\nMake sure to handle escaping: double quotes, backslashes, newlines as \\n. Use json.dumps on the python code string? Actually we need to construct JSON manually to ensure correct escaping. Better to write the code as a string variable in Python then use json.dumps to escape. But the response must be pure JSON. So I'll write the python code as a multi-line string, then manually escape for JSON or use a Python script mentally. I'll do it carefully.\n\nThe code:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case[\"nodes\"]\n    core = case[\"core_distance\"]\n    distances = case[\"distances\"]\n    # Build edges\n    edges = []\n    for u, v, d in distances:\n        if u > v:\n            u, v = v, u\n        mr = max(core[u], core[v], d)\n        edges.append((mr, u, v))\n    # Sort by weight, then by u, then v\n    edges.sort(key=lambda e: (e[0], e[1], e[2]))\n    # Union-Find\n    parent = {node: node for node in nodes}\n    rank = {node: 0 for node in nodes}\n    def find(x):\n        while parent[x] != x:\n            parent[x] = parent[parent[x]]\n            x = parent[x]\n        return x\n    def union(x, y):\n        rx, ry = find(x), find(y)\n        if rx == ry:\n            return False\n        if rank[rx] < rank[ry]:\n            parent[rx] = ry\n        elif rank[rx] > rank[ry]:\n            parent[ry] = rx\n        else:\n            parent[ry] = rx\n            rank[rx] += 1\n        return True\n    mst_edges = []\n    total_weight = 0.0\n    for mr, u, v in edges:\n        if union(u, v):\n            mst_edges.append([u, v, mr])\n            total_weight += mr\n            if len(mst_edges) == len(nodes) - 1:\n                break\n    if len(mst_edges) != len(nodes) - 1:\n        raise ValueError(\"Disconnected graph\")\n    return {\"mst_edges\": mst_edges, \"total_weight\": total_weight}\n```\n\nWe need to ensure no mutation of case. We don't mutate.\n\nNow escape for JSON: newlines become \\n, backslashes become \\\\, double quotes become \\\". Also indent? Not needed, but we can keep as a single line? Better to keep multi-line for readability but it's fine. We'll embed as a string.\n\nLet's produce the final JSON. I'll use a Python script mentally: code_str = ... then print json.dumps({\"implementation\": code_str}). But I'll do it manually.\n\nThe code string with escaped characters:\n\n\"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case[\\\"nodes\\\"]\\n    core = case[\\\"core_distance\\\"]\\n    distances = case[\\\"distances\\\"]\\n    edges = []\\n    for u, v, d in distances:\\n        if u > v:\\n            u, v = v, u\\n        mr = max(core[u], core[v], d)\\n        edges.append((mr, u, v))\\n    edges.sort(key=lambda e: (e[0], e[1], e[2]))\\n    parent = {node: node for node in nodes}\\n    rank = {node: 0 for node in nodes}\\n    def find(x):\\n        while parent[x] != x:\\n            parent[x] = parent[parent[x]]\\n            x = parent[x]\\n        return x\\n    def union(x, y):\\n        rx, ry = find(x), find(y)\\n        if rx == ry:\\n            return False\\n        if rank[rx] < rank[ry]:\\n            parent[rx] = ry\\n        elif rank[rx] > rank[ry]:\\n            parent[ry] = rx\\n        else:\\n            parent[ry] = rx\\n            rank[rx] += 1\\n        return True\\n    mst_edges = []\\n    total_weight = 0.0\\n    for mr, u, v in edges:\\n        if union(u, v):\\n            mst_edges.append([u, v, mr])\\n            total_weight += mr\\n            if len(mst_edges) == len(nodes) - 1:\\n                break\\n    if len(mst_edges) != len(nodes) - 1:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Disconnected graph\\\")\\n    return {\\\"mst_edges\\\": mst_edges, \\\"total_weight\\\": total_weight}\"\n\nWe need to escape backslashes: In the string there are backslashes? There are no backslashes in code except maybe in quotes? Actually there are no backslashes. But we have double quotes inside the string, which must be escaped with backslash. So replace every double quote with \\\". Also newline with \\n.\n\nThe code has double quotes: e.g., \"nodes\", \"core_distance\", \"distances\", etc. So we need to escape those. Let's rewrite the string with escaped double quotes.\n\nI'll produce the string by manually replacing.\n\nAlternatively, I can write the code without double quotes by using single quotes? But the code must be valid Python, can use single quotes for string literals. That would avoid escaping double quotes in the JSON value. Yes! Use single quotes in Python code. Then the JSON string will only need to escape backslashes and double quotes are not present (since we use single quotes). But single quotes need to be escaped only if inside single-quoted string? In JSON, single quotes are fine as characters. So I can write the code using single quotes for string literals. That makes escaping easier.\n\nLet's rewrite code with single quotes:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    core = case['core_distance']\n    distances = case['distances']\n    edges = []\n    for u, v, d in distances:\n        if u > v:\n            u, v = v, u\n        mr = max(core[u], core[v], d)\n        edges.append((mr, u, v))\n    edges.sort(key=lambda e: (e[0], e[1], e[2]))\n    parent = {node: node for node in nodes}\n    rank = {node: 0 for node in nodes}\n    def find(x):\n        while parent[x] != x:\n            parent[x] = parent[parent[x]]\n            x = parent[x]\n        return x\n    def union(x, y):\n        rx, ry = find(x), find(y)\n        if rx == ry:\n            return False\n        if rank[rx] < rank[ry]:\n            parent[rx] = ry\n        elif rank[rx] > rank[ry]:\n            parent[ry] = rx\n        else:\n            parent[ry] = rx\n            rank[rx] += 1\n        return True\n    mst_edges = []\n    total_weight = 0.0\n    for mr, u, v in edges:\n        if union(u, v):\n            mst_edges.append([u, v, mr])\n            total_weight += mr\n            if len(mst_edges) == len(nodes) - 1:\n                break\n    if len(mst_edges) != len(nodes) - 1:\n        raise ValueError('Disconnected graph')\n    return {'mst_edges': mst_edges, 'total_weight': total_weight}\n\nNow all string literals use single quotes. The JSON string value will contain single quotes and no double quotes. The only characters to escape are backslash and newline. There is no backslash in 